t{for any}$ $\\text{integer}$ $k,$ we have $15\\ne 8k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407492 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407503 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407505 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407526 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221908" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407526 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407526 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407526 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10c4f1e2-954f-5157-a52e-a25057a3e354" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407526 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$ So, $4∣16$ means $4\\text{ divides }16.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $4∣16$ is equivalent to the statement that there exists $\\text{an integer}$ $k$ such that $16=4k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407504 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407521 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407522 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407527 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221914" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407527 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407527 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407527 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1931ec0b-024e-521b-8944-a44a623f0a94" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407527 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∤b$ means $a$ does not divides $b.$ So, $3∤5$ means $3\\text{ doesn't divide }5.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb\\ne ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3∤5$ is equivalent to the statement that $\\text{for any}$ $\\text{integer}$ $k,$ we have $5\\ne 3k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407494 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407496 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407524 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221913" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407528 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "327fb10a-fdb6-5fde-b81d-bde8d0149385" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407528 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∤b$ means $a$ does not divides $b.$ So, $5∤9$ means $5\\text{ doesn't divide }9.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb\\ne ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $5∤9$ is equivalent to the statement that $\\text{for any}$ $\\text{integer}$ $k,$ we have $9\\ne 5k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407529 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407489 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407529 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407529 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407529 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407508 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407529 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221916" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407529 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407529 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407529 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a21913b-5a41-5760-aaf4-e4ed518c2eae" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407529 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∤b$ means $a$ does not divides $b.$ So, $6∤11$ means $6\\text{ doesn't divide }11.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb\\ne ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $6∤11$ is equivalent to the statement that $\\text{for any}$ $\\text{integer}$ $k,$ we have $11\\ne 6k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407530 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407515 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407530 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221924" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407530 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407530 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $2∣(60x-45y)$\n- $9∣(60x-45y)$\n- $5∣(60x-45y)$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57bcbc75-d195-518f-bc0e-7dcf10346726" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407530 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(0)-45(1)=-45,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(1)-45(1)=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n60x-45y=5(12x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $12x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $5∣(60x-45y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407531 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407511 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407531 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221927" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407531 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407531 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $5∣(8x+6y)$\n- $2∣(8x+6y)$\n- $3∣(8x+6y)$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "677c3e63-2822-579f-a67a-69517e4d5770" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407531 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $8(1)+6(0)=8,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n8x+6y=2(4x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $4x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2∣(8x+6y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $8(1)+6(0)=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407502 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407532 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221928" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407532 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407532 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $3$ divides $(8x+6y)$\n- $2$ divides $(8x+6y)$\n- $4$ divides $(8x+6y)$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87475b8f-4c6f-5041-abc0-cc4d71389ee6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407532 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $8(1)+6(0)=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n8x+6y=2(4x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $4x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2$ divides $(8x+6y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $8(0)+6(1)=6,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407490 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407506 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407533 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221915" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407533 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407533 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407533 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d23d47f-619a-511e-9eb8-d16f783f2804" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407533 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∤b$ means $a$ does not divides $b.$ So, $7∤8$ means $7\\text{ doesn't divide }8.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb\\ne ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $7∤8$ is equivalent to the statement that $\\text{for any}$ $\\text{integer}$ $k,$ we have $8\\ne 7k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407519 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407520 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407523 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221902" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407534 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac419645-38ad-5793-9dc4-addab4a7d9d8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407534 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$ So, $4∣12$ means $4\\text{ divides }12.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $4∣12$ is equivalent to the statement that there exists $\\text{an integer}$ $k$ such that $12=4k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407513 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407514 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407518 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221906" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407535 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b769e8d9-7e36-5efc-b6ab-edadbe2e852d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407535 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$ So, $8∣16$ means $8\\text{ divides }16.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $8∣16$ is equivalent to the statement that there exists $\\text{an integer}$ $k$ such that $16=8k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407507 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407512 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407516 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407536 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221901" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407536 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407536 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0842d5b-e854-58d5-ba21-604088a32084" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407536 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$ So, $5∣10$ means $5\\text{ divides }10.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $5∣10$ is equivalent to the statement that there exists $\\text{an integer}$ $k$ such that $10=5k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407537 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407510 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407537 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221932" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407537 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407537 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $6$ divides $(24x+30y)$\n- $3$ divides $(24x+30y)$\n- $5$ divides $(24x+30y)$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407537 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7df593d-9767-5ee4-9c1c-cf41d12d4aeb" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407537 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n24x+30y=6(4x+5y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $4x+5y$ must be an integer, too. So, $6$ divides $(24x+30y).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n24x+30y=3(8x+10y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $8x+10y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3$ divides $(24x+30y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have $24(-1)+30(1)=6,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585]}
